{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1213 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "17e131ef31847faffb83f71bb68c835a51703cb0edd2909f59a723a563e7fc95" :edb/db-before-t 1212 :edb/db-after-t 1213 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534525 50 #inst "2026-10-07T20:20:20.632Z" 13194139534525 true] [17592186175679 1100 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$s(t) = -\\frac{1}{3}t^3 + \\frac{5}{2}t^2 + 6t + 1,\\quad t\\geq 0$.\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary, and give the displacement of the particle from the origin at this time." 13194139534525 false] [17592186175679 1100 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t) = -\\frac{1}{3}t^3 + \\frac{5}{2}t^2 + 6t + 1,\\quad t\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary, and give the displacement of the particle from the origin at this time." 13194139534525 true] [17592186175679 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{ds}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(-\\frac{1}{3}t^{3} + \\frac{5}{2}t^{2} + 6t + 1) \\\\\n&=-t^{2} + 5t + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when\n\n$$\nv=0\\, \\textrm{m/s}\n$$\n\n. So, we set $v = 0$ and solve for $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= -t^{2} + 5t + 6 \\\\\n0 &= t^{2} - 5t - 6 \\\\\n0 &= (t - 6)(t + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=6$ and $t=-1$. Since\n\n$$\nt\\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe discard the solution $t=-1$ and keep only $t=6$.\n\nSo the particle is stationary only at\n\n$$\nt=6\\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nTo find the displacement from the origin at this time, we substitute $t=6$ back into $s(t)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns(6) &= -\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 6^{3} + \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot 6^{2} + 6 \\cdot 6 + 1 \\\\\n&=-72 + 90 + 36 + 1 \\\\\n&= 55m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at\n\n$$\nt = 6\\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nand its displacement from the origin at this time is $55\\, \\textrm{m}$." 13194139534525 false] [17592186175679 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}+6t+1) \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}+5t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\, \\textrm{m/s}$. So, we set $v = 0$ and solve for $t \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-{t}^{2}+5t+6 \\\\ 0 & ={t}^{2}-5t-6 \\\\ 0 & =(t-6)(t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=6$ and $t=-1.$ Since $t\\geq 0,$ we discard the solution $t=-1$ and keep only $t=6.$\n\nSo the particle is stationary only at $t=6\\, \\textrm{s}.$ To find the displacement from the origin at this time, we substitute $t=6$ back into $s(t)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {6}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}+6\\cdot 6+1 \\\\  & =-72+90+36+1 \\\\  & =55\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t = 6\\, \\textrm{s}$ and its displacement from the origin at this time is $55\\, \\textrm{m}.$" 13194139534525 true] [17592186175680 1100 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t) = t^3-3t^2+12t,\\quad t\\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." 13194139534525 true] [17592186175680 1100 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n$s(t) = t^3-3t^2+12t,\\quad t\\geq 0$.\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." 13194139534525 false] [17592186175680 1145 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{ds}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3} - 3t^{2} + 12t) \\\\\n&= 3t^{2} - 6t + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(3t^{2} - 6t + 12) \\\\\n&= 6t - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= 6t - 6 \\\\\n6t &= 6 \\\\\nt &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1$. To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1) = 3(1)^2 - 6(1)+12 = 9\n$$" 13194139534525 false] [17592186175680 1145 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+12t) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-6t+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-6t+12) \\\\  & =6t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6t-6 \\\\ 6t & =6 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1$. To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1) = 3(1)^2 - 6(1)+12 = 9.\n$$" 13194139534525 true] [17592186175712 1100 "The displacement $s$, in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t) = t^4 - 32t$, where $t \\geq 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$." 13194139534525 false] [17592186175712 1100 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t) = t^4 - 32t$, where $t \\geq 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" 13194139534525 true] [17592186175712 1145 "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x$, then its velocity is negative.\n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{ds}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{4} - 32t) \\\\\n&= 4t^{3} - 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t) < 0$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}v(t) & < 0 \\\\ 4t^{3} - 32 & < 0 \\\\ 4t^{3} & < 32 \\\\ t^{3} & < 8 \\\\ t & < 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $[MATH: t\\in\\left[0, 2\\right).]$" 13194139534525 false] [17592186175712 1145 "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-32t) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t) <    0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3}-32 & <0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3} & <32 \\\\ {t}^{3} & <8 \\\\ t & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in\\left[ 0, 2\\right).$" 13194139534525 true] [17592186175863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293147 13194139534525 true] [17592186175863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293152 13194139534525 true] [17592186175863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293169 13194139534525 true] [17592186175863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293176 13194139534525 true] [17592186175863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293197 13194139534525 true] [17592186176018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293112 13194139534525 true] [17592186176018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293119 13194139534525 true] [17592186176018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293126 13194139534525 true] [17592186176018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293162 13194139534525 true] [17592186176018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293190 13194139534525 true] [17592186176021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293133 13194139534525 true] [17592186176021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293140 13194139534525 true] [17592186176021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293157 13194139534525 true] [17592186176021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293183 13194139534525 true] [17592186176486 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534525 true] [17592186176486 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n$s(t) = t^{2}\\ln (t), t \\ge 0$.\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." 13194139534525 false] [17592186176486 1100 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." 13194139534525 true] [17592186176486 1145 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+3 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & ={e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t={e}^{-3/2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v({e}^{-3/2}) & =2({e}^{-3/2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-3/2})+{e}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3/2}(-\\frac{3}{2})+{e}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293112 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293113 13194139534525 true] [17592186293112 1089 "q-109274" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293112 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534525 true] [17592186293112 1100 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=(t-3){e}^{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." 13194139534525 true] [17592186293112 1113 #uuid "25e83974-7897-5d7f-96f2-7e49663e3f50" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293112 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-3){e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+(t-3){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{t} & =0 \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293114 13194139534525 true] [17592186293113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293115 13194139534525 true] [17592186293113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293116 13194139534525 true] [17592186293113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293117 13194139534525 true] [17592186293113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293118 13194139534525 true] [17592186293113 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534525 true] [17592186293113 1041 "selection" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293113 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293117 13194139534525 true] [17592186293113 1110 #uuid "35640171-26d5-56b7-8531-903070905429" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293114 1034 "t=3\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293114 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293114 1111 #uuid "c8cd8a91-5381-568a-bbfe-1f703ad6407d" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293115 1034 "t=2{e}^{2}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293115 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293115 1111 #uuid "b40340f9-c466-514f-884a-2e7d7b67a2de" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293116 1034 "t=3{e}^{2}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293116 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293116 1111 #uuid "4b609f51-6620-5550-84a4-565df43107d8" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293117 1034 "t=2\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293117 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293117 1111 #uuid "59bf99a4-4487-5b03-b2c0-5ba248cdffc5" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293118 1034 "t=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293118 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293118 1111 #uuid "49a8aa83-c4a2-589e-842a-b85f4529b97f" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293119 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293120 13194139534525 true] [17592186293119 1089 "q-109275" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293119 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534525 true] [17592186293119 1100 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-6t+10),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." 13194139534525 true] [17592186293119 1113 #uuid "a2e365c2-fe58-52a7-96c4-f4db359dbf61" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293119 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-6t+10)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-6}{{t}^{2}-6t+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t-3)}{{t}^{2}-6t+10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(t-3)}{{t}^{2}-6t+10} & =0 \\\\ 2(t-3) & =0 \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293120 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293121 13194139534525 true] [17592186293120 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293122 13194139534525 true] [17592186293120 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293123 13194139534525 true] [17592186293120 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293124 13194139534525 true] [17592186293120 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293125 13194139534525 true] [17592186293120 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534525 true] [17592186293120 1041 "selection" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293120 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293121 13194139534525 true] [17592186293120 1110 #uuid "b14b2892-93f5-53db-aabb-53470c6190ed" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293121 1034 "t=3\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293121 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293121 1111 #uuid "b4bb3f7f-3469-5405-897c-ae2bd7b7985a" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293122 1034 "t=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293122 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293122 1111 #uuid "713f8e69-adfe-5c71-91a2-a882cc7523a1" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293123 1034 "t=1\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293123 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293123 1111 #uuid "53df5b51-d99c-5a79-819b-3a94533a12c4" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293124 1034 "t=2\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293124 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293124 1111 #uuid "5b5fdd4f-b41c-552d-8974-8eaffca85c40" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293125 1034 "t=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293125 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293125 1111 #uuid "cc6ec469-cd59-593b-bc0a-a723d4abd25f" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293126 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293127 13194139534525 true] [17592186293126 1089 "q-109276" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293126 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534525 true] [17592186293126 1100 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." 13194139534525 true] [17592186293126 1113 #uuid "d098c0db-a314-5213-9701-dea3457fb68f" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293126 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t \\\\  & =t(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1) & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293128 13194139534525 true] [17592186293127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293129 13194139534525 true] [17592186293127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293130 13194139534525 true] [17592186293127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293131 13194139534525 true] [17592186293127 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293132 13194139534525 true] [17592186293127 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534525 true] [17592186293127 1041 "selection" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293127 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293132 13194139534525 true] [17592186293127 1110 #uuid "3130adfc-ef2a-5b80-b47c-71c848fca0b3" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293128 1034 "t=1\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293128 1111 #uuid "a21dfbdd-deb0-5415-a30b-f58eaa37838a" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293129 1034 "t=e\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293129 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293129 1111 #uuid "1b591117-c147-5078-bb26-0bc06b59aa5a" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293130 1034 "t=\\sqrt{e}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293130 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293130 1111 #uuid "91f98767-8bd1-536f-9465-8fe19115b782" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293131 1034 "t=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{e}}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293131 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293131 1111 #uuid "cbcbc8b2-e2e2-5466-a60b-ff17c0cc6836" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293132 1034 "t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293132 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293132 1111 #uuid "846c852f-b19d-5eb0-9c5f-6a43fa64007d" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293133 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293134 13194139534525 true] [17592186293133 1089 "q-109302" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293133 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534525 true] [17592186293133 1100 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=2{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+3t-2,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." 13194139534525 true] [17592186293133 1113 #uuid "6414dac4-9443-5e38-b246-ae2ca315e478" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293133 1145 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+3t-2) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-18t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-18t+3) \\\\  & =12t-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12t-18 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{18}{12} \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{3}{2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{3}{2}) & =6{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-18(\\frac{3}{2})+3 \\\\  & =6(\\frac{9}{4})-27+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-27+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293135 13194139534525 true] [17592186293134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293136 13194139534525 true] [17592186293134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293137 13194139534525 true] [17592186293134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293138 13194139534525 true] [17592186293134 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293139 13194139534525 true] [17592186293134 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534525 true] [17592186293134 1041 "selection" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293134 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293136 13194139534525 true] [17592186293134 1110 #uuid "7dc3cc59-7e89-5c2c-824f-cbadf0f09f87" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293135 1034 "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{15}{2}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293135 1111 #uuid "de7a8620-0953-5a2a-99bd-578e05b45ce2" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293136 1034 "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{21}{2}" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293136 1111 #uuid "bd259a28-0f07-56f4-99b3-cc005d451f06" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293137 1034 "t=1,\\,v=-7" 13194139534525 true] [17592186293137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534525 true] [17592186293137 1111 #uuid "